WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.935
Back to the kitchen.

2
00:00:03.935 --> 00:00:07.518
Waiters clip tickets to the rail, and cooks take them off.

3
00:00:07.518 --> 00:00:14.560
In code, each waiter and each cook is a thread, a path of work running beside the others, and the rail is a queue.

4
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:17.256
A thread that puts work in is a producer.

5
00:00:17.256 --> 00:00:19.867
A thread that takes work out is a consumer.

6
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:23.693
One side makes work at one speed, the other does it at another.

7
00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:26.121
Most programs have this shape somewhere.

8
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:28.333
Java's rail is a BlockingQueue.

9
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:33.043
put adds a ticket, and take removes one, waiting while the rail is empty.

10
00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:35.366
Give it a limit, here room for four.

11
00:00:35.366 --> 00:00:43.883
When the rail is full, put waits too, so a fast waiter slows to the cooks' pace, instead of piling up tickets until memory runs out.

12
00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:46.335
That push back is called backpressure.

13
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:47.854
Now it's closing time.

14
00:00:47.854 --> 00:00:52.771
The waiters have stopped, a few tickets are left, and three cooks work through them.

15
00:00:52.771 --> 00:00:56.517
Then the rail is empty, and each cook waits for the next ticket.

16
00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:58.156
Tonight, there won't be one.

17
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:01.492
So here's a question that sounds too simple to ask.

18
00:01:01.492 --> 00:01:04.801
Why can't a cook just see the empty rail, and go home?

19
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:07.071
Because empty doesn't mean over.

20
00:01:07.071 --> 00:01:09.681
It can also mean the next ticket is late.

21
00:01:09.681 --> 00:01:15.156
And a cook waiting in take is asleep on the bench, like in the wait and notify lesson.

22
00:01:15.156 --> 00:01:20.313
Only a put rings its bell, and once the waiters have gone home, nobody ever will.

23
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:25.686
The quickest fix is to interrupt every cook: stop, right now.

24
00:01:25.686 --> 00:01:26.285
It's fast.

25
00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:32.991
But take checks for the interrupt before it looks at the rail, so the cooks leave with tickets still clipped up.

26
00:01:32.991 --> 00:01:36.823
Three cooks go home, and table 12 is still waiting for its soup.

27
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:41.237
Or each cook could check every second: anything yet, and should I stop?

28
00:01:41.237 --> 00:01:47.393
That works, but a cook may wait a whole second to notice, and every loop must check the flag just right.

29
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.716
The clean answer is a card on the rail that says: kitchen closed.

30
00:01:51.716 --> 00:01:55.894
It's called a poison pill, a special item that means no more work.

31
00:01:55.894 --> 00:01:57.604
A cook that takes it stops.

32
00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:03.293
The head chef waits until both waiters are done, then clips one card to the rail.

33
00:02:03.293 --> 00:02:04.102
Three cooks.

34
00:02:04.102 --> 00:02:04.979
What happens?

35
00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:10.311
One cook takes the card and goes home.

36
00:02:10.311 --> 00:02:13.088
The card was just an item, and now it's gone.

37
00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:15.741
The other two find an empty rail, and wait.

38
00:02:15.741 --> 00:02:20.060
In the demo, a second later, two cooks were still WAITING inside take.

39
00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.256
And whatever waits for them, like the program's shutdown, hangs too.

40
00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:27.077
So, one card per cook.

41
00:02:27.077 --> 00:02:32.027
Three cooks, three cards, and each cook stops at its own.

42
00:02:32.027 --> 00:02:36.457
The demo's cooks handled 511, 177 and 1,312 orders.

43
00:02:36.457 --> 00:02:42.623
That's 2,000, every order the waiters put, and nobody was left waiting.

44
00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:44.714
Three rules make pills work.

45
00:02:44.714 --> 00:02:50.557
One: put them only after every producer is done, so they land behind every real ticket.

46
00:02:50.557 --> 00:02:54.587
Two: one pill per consumer, because each pill is taken once.

47
00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:01.935
Three: recognize the pill by identity, with == in Java and === in Kotlin, not by what it says.

48
00:03:01.935 --> 00:03:06.337
A real ticket that happens to read PILL is still a ticket.

49
00:03:06.337 --> 00:03:08.918
Somebody ordered the pill special.

50
00:03:08.918 --> 00:03:09.525
Cook it.

51
00:03:10.333 --> 00:03:13.770
The lesson has two more classics, and you've met both.

52
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:20.643
Five philosophers at a round table each pick up the fork on their left, and wait for the one on their right.

53
00:03:20.643 --> 00:03:23.570
That's Ana and Ben's circle, with five people.

54
00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:29.234
In the demo they ate 0 of 1,000 meals, and with the same cure, one lock order, all 1,000.

55
00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:34.580
And readers who keep a price board busy can starve the owner who wants to change it.

56
00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.647
Here are the pills in code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

57
00:03:39.647 --> 00:03:42.541
Each cook takes tickets until it gets its pill.

58
00:03:42.541 --> 00:03:46.727
The head chef puts one pill per cook, after the last waiter is done.

59
00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:51.010
So, why can't a cook go home when the rail is empty?

60
00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.350
Because empty might only mean late.

61
00:03:53.350 --> 00:04:00.972
Send the end of work down the rail itself: one pill per consumer, after the last producer, recognized by identity.

62
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:01.946
Quick check.

63
00:04:01.946 --> 00:04:07.542
The head chef puts the three pills on the rail while one waiter is still clipping tickets.

64
00:04:07.542 --> 00:04:08.537
What goes wrong?
